WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.290
- Hey guys, welcome to a new episode

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:03.740
of the Wednesday Checkup.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.990
Today I'm going to be
sharing a story with you

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:07.680
that happened a couple of weeks ago,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.960
where unfortunately a patient
filed a complaint against me.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.840
That made me really emotional.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:13.708
Let me tell you why.

00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:17.125
(light electronic music)

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.540
Before we start I want to let you know

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.610
I'm going to be changing some
details about the patient.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.000
Just so we maintain this person's privacy,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.940
and we don't make anybody
feel uncomfortable.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.910
Because that is not the
goal of this channel.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.250
Our job is to stay happy,
healthy, learn about ourselves.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.130
Learn from our mistakes, and
that's what we're going to do

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:36.963
with today's story.

00:00:36.963 --> 00:00:38.210
Here we go, let's get started.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.330
To set the scene,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.100
I'm working in my outpatient
family medicine office,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.280
and I see a patient on my schedule.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.610
I have the demographics that he's a male,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.160
he's in his late 20's,
and also the complaint

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.120
or reason why he made the appointment.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.730
Patient would like MRI for low back pain.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.670
Patient comes in, my
nurse rooms the patient,

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.250
I go in and we start
having a conversation.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.410
The history of present
illness goes as follows:

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.830
The patient is a late 20s
male, works many hours

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.200
in his job as a department store salesman,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.210
where he's on his feet.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.480
He's been complaining of low back pain

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.240
for an extended period of time.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.300
Worse sometimes, getting better sometimes.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.480
Currently is not in a lot of pain,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.400
somewhere like three to five out of 10.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.830
It does get pretty bad at times,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.380
going all the way up to an eight.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.130
This patient has had prior back surgery.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.850
We don't really have a clear understanding

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.380
as to what the surgery was

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.170
and what the indication
for the surgery was.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.690
But we know there was a
back surgery performed.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.120
And my first questions is,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.140
because my goal is to help the patient

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.400
and answer the patient's
question directly,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.860
was, you told me, or at
least you told my nurse,

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.070
that you would like an MRI,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.290
what are you looking to
find out with the MRI?

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.690
He wants an MRI, because he's
worried that the activities

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.960
he's doing in his everyday
life, could be causing

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.340
more damage to his low back,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.150
and that perhaps there may be scar tissue

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.300
from the past surgery, or
perhaps there's something

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.760
else going on within his
lumbar, or his thoracic spine

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.310
that could be causing his symptoms.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.870
I understood where he was coming from,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.260
so I said, Okay, let's take
that apart piece by piece

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:11.410
and we'll have a longer conversation

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.710
than we originally scheduled.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.890
The visit was supposed to be 15 minutes,

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.330
which is normally what we
get for an acute visit,

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:18.930
but I said, we'll spend extra time,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.210
because I really want us to
both be on the same page,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.420
and especially because
I've never seen you before,

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.440
I wanna create a little
bit of a relationship

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.470
before giving any kind
of advice or guidance.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.010
One of my first questions,
being sports medicine

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.830
and osteopathic medicine trained, was,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.900
do you do any kind of physical activities

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.830
and how does your back pain affect that?

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.380
He told me that every morning,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.110
or pretty much five days out of the week,

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.560
he does calisthenics in the morning

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.540
for about half an hour,
and he's able to do those

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.530
pain free, and actually,
he gets a lot of benefit

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.290
from doing this
calisthenics and stretching.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.120
Also, at least once a week

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.780
he goes on an eight/nine mile hike.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.450
After the eighth or ninth
mile he starts developing

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.660
low back pain that makes
it uncomfortable for him

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.010
to continue and he has
to cut his hike short.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.920
What do I take, as a doctor,
from that kind of information?

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.210
Well, first it shows me

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.960
that this patient is very functional,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.370
that they're able to do
stretches, they're able to do

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.410
complicated and compound movements.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:12.500
They're not limited
incredibly by this back pain.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.010
That was a good sign for
me, because as a doctor,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.480
my job is always to
get my patients moving,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.240
especially when it comes to low back pain.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.320
There was a misconception
back in the day that,

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.370
if your low back hurts,
you should rest it.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.850
That's actually pretty inaccurate,

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.210
because, what we found is,
the longer time you spend

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.460
in bed with low back pain,
the worse it can actually get.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.860
Now, this was my first
time seeing the patient,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.130
so I asked, what's going
on in my patients life.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.270
He described a very stressful life,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.880
especially when it comes to work.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.000
He's working many hours,
12 to 14 hours at times,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.890
and the back pain gets worse
after standing all day.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.690
And, at times, the patient
even became tearful,

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.270
because he was so upset about
the stressors in his life,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.830
of family, friends, there
was a lot of things going on,

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:57.240
but I could see that stress
was a major component,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.150
and there was a big mental
component of this pain.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.310
So after getting the full history

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:04.780
and what was going on with his back pain,

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.170
I perform a physical exam.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.460
Now, in the physical exam the
patient had some muscle spams,

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.490
straight leg raise, which
is a test that we do

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.720
to check for sciatica,
was actually negative,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:14.680
which is a good sign.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.620
That means, that the sciatic
nerve is not involved

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.630
in this type of pain.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.380
I did some other provocative tests

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.590
and everything came up negative.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.000
There were some tender
points that I elicited,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.770
but nothing that was really worrisome,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.630
no bony tenderness,
the reflexes were fine,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.460
those are important
things that we rule out

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.200
when we're looking at someones back pain.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.300
So, once I conducted the proper history,

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.680
then I did the full physical.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.640
I then decided to explain to the patient

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.970
what my thoughts are on
whether or not he needs an MRI.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.860
I told the patient that an
MRI wouldn't benefit him,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.640
because what we find on MRIs,
doesn't always translate

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.290
to clinical symptoms, in fact,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.510
it doesn't the majority of the time.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.000
There have been studies done
in the past, where we scanned

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.930
100 people who have no back pain,

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.220
and found that on MRIs, they
had significant findings.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.450
Arthritic changes, degenerative
changes, bulging discs.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.160
But these patients
weren't having back pain.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.370
So what does that tell us?

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.334
That tells us, that just
because you have a finding

00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:10.400
on the MRI, it doesn't mean

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.120
that it'll have a clinical significance.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.480
And as a doctor, it's not
my job to treat the MRI,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.650
it's to treat my patient.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.410
So I told the patient that based
on how functional they are,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.830
based on my physical exam,
knowing that they're able

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.970
to do calisthenics everyday,
hike for eight miles,

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.390
I can barely hike for eight miles,

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:30.010
their back pain seemed more
tied to their stress levels

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:30.880
in their job.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.770
Now, that's not to say my
patient doesn't have a true

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.340
medical problem, they do,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.130
and it's elicited by tender points.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.580
They're describing pain,
which is a subjective measure,

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.820
but they are describing
it and they're saying

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.370
how uncomfortable they are.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:45.950
So, it's my job to fix that.

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.460
So what I explained to the patient, is,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.040
I thought that they could
benefit from conservative

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.410
management, specifically physical therapy.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.160
I also recommended some
osteopathic manipulation

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.760
and I gave a recommendation
of some reading material

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.770
about mind-body conditions,
where based on our mental state,

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.820
some physical problems can
arise, like low back pain.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.270
As a primary care doctor, it's
my job to delay the process

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.970
of risky procedures as much as I can,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.200
as long as there's no
pressing matters at hand.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.670
I didn't view that an MRI
was the correct next step.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.780
But, I understand that some patients

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.350
may have reservations about that.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.620
I understand that they can have anxieties

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.050
about their back pain, for example,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.410
why is it there, I would
like to get a better look.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.860
So I explain that to the patient.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.970
If you don't agree with my evaluation,

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:32.580
or you think that you still need

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.870
an MRI after this discussion,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.320
I'll happily write you a
prescription for an MRI,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.000
and we can get the process started.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.150
Or, I'd like to see you
back the following week,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.140
so we can continue this conversation.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.010
You would have started
physical therapy by now

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.500
and gotten a couple of sessions in,

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:49.840
and we could see what
progress you're making.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.690
And if, during that point,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.030
you decide that you want an MRI,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.520
I'll write you the MRI then.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.370
I'll write you a referral to a specialist

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.280
if that's what you want,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.047
and my patient, seemed
to be very understanding.

00:07:00.047 --> 00:07:02.057
"Doctor, I appreciate
you spending the time.

00:07:02.057 --> 00:07:03.447
"I see what you're saying.

00:07:03.447 --> 00:07:05.537
"I don't want the MRI now, I hear you.

00:07:05.537 --> 00:07:07.565
"I'll see you next week,
I'll start physical therapy,

00:07:07.565 --> 00:07:09.067
"and we'll go from there."

00:07:09.067 --> 00:07:11.040
And I was really proud of myself.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.250
As a doctor, it's not always
easy to have a patient walk in

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:17.870
who wants something, and it's
your job to convince them

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.800
for their own good, or at least
from your medical opinion,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.640
for their own good, that
it's not ideal what they want

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:24.800
and that there's a better way.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.216
And, for them to understand
that and be on the same page,

00:07:27.216 --> 00:07:29.890
that takes skill and
sometimes it takes many years

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.640
for doctors to develop that skill.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.830
I came back the following
week to my hospital,

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.520
a patient called and left a complaint

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.220
with our office manager.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:40.950
The complaint was basically as follows.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.130
That, the doctor that I
saw, did not help me at all

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.060
with my pain.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.750
This doctor was overly
spiritual and I'd like to see

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.280
a real doctor, who's willing
to help me with my pain.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.890
It was something along those
lines, I'm paraphrasing.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.380
The rest of the day I
was left feeling like,

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.880
what could of I've done better?

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.480
What did I do wrong?

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:01.280
Where did the communication break down?

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.860
So I started replaying the situation

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:03.900
over and over in my head,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.910
trying to figure out what I can do better,

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.040
how I can improve.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.660
And, something that I do is, I ask people,

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.500
who have more experience than myself,

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.180
or people who are experts in
other fields, for their advice.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:17.370
So I went to go see one of
our behavioral specialists

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:18.910
that work within our office,

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.130
and basically what he told me is,

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.370
there are some patients,

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.380
which is a small percentage of patients,

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.400
like, less than 10%, even
less than 5% probably,

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:28.860
the more care you give them,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.430
the more you time you spend with them,

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.920
the more effort you try and go through

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:35.210
in explaining something,
the more that they take away

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:36.930
from that, something negative.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:38.880
Why is this doctor trying
to sell me on this?

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:39.770
What is the doctor?

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.590
Is he trying to get over me on this?

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:43.150
Especially if you're disagreeing

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:44.660
what they initially came in for.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.807
And he said, "What's a
good strategy for that,

00:08:46.807 --> 00:08:48.797
"basically, going into the patients room,

00:08:48.797 --> 00:08:51.447
"now that you know that their
personality style and type,

00:08:51.447 --> 00:08:52.967
"and just being very buttoned up.

00:08:52.967 --> 00:08:54.147
"Well, you want an MRI,

00:08:54.147 --> 00:08:56.867
"I don't think it's in your
best interests to get an MRI.

00:08:56.867 --> 00:08:58.837
"And say that, you know, if
you don't agree with this,

00:08:58.837 --> 00:09:01.684
"here's, I can give you a
referral to see a specialist,

00:09:01.684 --> 00:09:03.827
"another doctor, or the test

00:09:03.827 --> 00:09:05.320
"that you actually want ordered."

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.410
What it taught me was
that at certain times,

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:11.330
with certain personalities, it
makes sense to be more brief,

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.510
and to be more succinct and
just explain things clearly,

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.546
instead of trying to sell
and influence that person,

00:09:17.546 --> 00:09:20.250
'cause it can come off
manipulative to some people.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.600
You can't fault yourself for everything.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:26.410
A situation could just happen
because of an unfortunate

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:27.770
coincidence, if you will.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.510
The fact that this patient never met me,

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:32.350
I never met the patient,
I tried to be really nice,

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:35.040
the patient misinterpreted
that, for whatever reason.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.400
The patient filed the complaint,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.320
but the people that
know me and the nurses,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:39.280
and other patients,

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.490
will understand that my
intentions were good.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:42.930
Intentions matter.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.450
There's many times where
we have great intentions,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.600
but have bad outcomes.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.940
We need to always look at the intentions

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:50.150
and then look at the outcomes.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.600
If the intentions were bad,
now we know that person

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.570
needs some work on that
end of the spectrum.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:55.733
If the intentions were good,

00:09:55.733 --> 00:09:58.340
but the outcomes were bad,
then we need to work on

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.453
the action and figure out
what we need to improve

00:10:00.453 --> 00:10:01.670
on that action.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:03.250
I wanna keep learning as a doctor,

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:05.200
and I feel like, if we can learn together,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.530
along on this unique journey of mine,

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:09.430
I think that's even more
special and that's why

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:10.610
